#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tuxwall-tripwire - WAN containment on confirmed intrusion-class events.

Configuration (/etc/tuxwall/tripwire.json, schema version 2, root 0600):
  enabled   master switch; off = no detection threads run and nothing can
            cut the network (a trip already in force holds until resolved)
  strength  block strength used by every check whose action is Block:
    medium  trip -> all forwarding cut (FORWARD drop, v4 + v6); the
            router's own listeners (WireGuard 51820 included) stay up
    high    trip -> forwarding cut + new WAN INPUT closed; the router is
            reachable from the LAN only. WAN DHCP (v4 67/68, v6 546/547),
            IPv6 neighbour discovery and replies to connections the
            router itself opened stay allowed, so the WAN lease survives
            and pushes/recovery keep working
  notify    {"on_log": push log-only events, "window_minutes": at most one
            push per check per window (later events are summarised)}
  checks    per check: action off | log | block, threshold {count, minutes}
            (N events within M minutes), exclusions {ips (CIDRs, matched
            against source OR destination), sids (Suricata signature ids),
            wan_only (Suricata: only internet-sourced traffic counts)}
  mode      DERIVED, written for older readers: disabled | logonly |
            medium | high (= what the strongest enabled check would do)
A version-1 file ({"mode"}) is migrated in place at daemon start: logonly,
medium and high become master on + the existing checks on Log only (never
Block) with the old strength kept; disabled (or no file) = every check Off.

Checks (CHECKS registry):
  suricata.<class>  Suricata priority-1 alerts in the exploit-success
     classes successful-admin, attempted-admin, trojan-activity,
     web-application-attack, shellcode-detect
     - BUT ONLY when the attack landed on something REAL. Events whose
       destination is a live decoy - the WAN address a canary/tarpit
       listens on AND one of its configured ports (read from
       honeypot.json / opencanary.conf, never a fixed list) - are delegated:
       the honeypot absorbed the attack and the ban pipeline owns the
       response - the tripwire never contains for them (a Mirai scan
       hitting the fake ADB port is weather, not an incident). They are
       counted as deception-absorbed and remain visible in the log.
  canary.egress  the opencanary uid attempts ANY outbound connection
     beyond its webhook and DNS (kernel log prefix
     [TUXWALL-CANARY-EGRESS]) - presumed deception-layer compromise

Always ignored (excluded, recorded in the history with the reason):
Suricata alerts sourced from the router itself or loopback, and canary
"egress" that never left the box (OUT=lo / loopback destination).

Event history: every evaluated event except Off checks is appended to
/var/lib/tuxwall/tripwire-events.jsonl (90 days, 5 MB cap) by the
record_event hook. `test CHECK` runs a synthetic event through the same
evaluation with no push, no enforcement and no history entry.

No auto-restore, ever: a trip holds until an administrator resolves it
deliberately (a timer would re-open the door before the compromise is
actually fixed; a CrowdSec ban does not un-find the way in).

Recovery mode (Tripped -> Recovery -> Restored): while tripped an admin may
start a time-boxed investigation window (default 60 min, extendable to at
most 240 min left, persisted; expiry or `recovery-end` returns to the full
trip, a new blocking event ends it too). The trip's rules stay; the
router's own traffic out of the WAN is narrowed to an allowlist (HTTPS to
the AI providers configured in opencode, opencode inference and the push
targets; ipsets tw-recovery4/6 refreshed every 5 min), DNS leaves only from
the resolver's uid and unbound answers only the allowlisted names (runtime
local-zones, re-applied after an unbound restart). LAN/loopback model
servers are reported as local models (reachable = no internet needed).
`restore` is the dashboard's resolve: checklist + note, audited.

State survives reboot: tripwire-state.json + the daemon re-enforces the
lockdown at service start (ExecStartPre) and every 15 s while tripped, so
neither a reboot nor a ufw reload can drop the containment.

Health: every watcher thread beats; the daemon writes
/run/tuxwall-tripwire/health.json every few seconds (the dashboard shows it
and raises an incident when a watcher is dead or stale).

Testing: TUXWALL_TRIPWIRE_ROOT=<dir> remaps every path under <dir> and
stubs enforcement (rules are recorded in <dir>/enforce.log) and the unbound
restart; adding TUXWALL_TRIPWIRE_NETNS=<ns> applies the real rules inside
that network namespace instead. TUXWALL_TRIPWIRE_PUSH_FILE=<file> records
pushes instead of sending them. TUXWALL_TRIPWIRE_OWN_IPS / _LAN_NETS
(comma lists) replace the router's addresses / internal networks that are
otherwise read from `ip -o addr`; _WAN_IPS lists the WAN addresses (decoy
destinations) in that mode. Recovery: TUXWALL_TRIPWIRE_UNBOUND_CONTROL
(a test unbound's control command; in a sandbox without it the DNS
restriction is only recorded), _DNS (resolver ip[#port]), _FAKE_DNS (JSON
host -> addresses), _RESOLVER_USER, _RECOVERY_REFRESH. Never point a test
at the live firewall or the live unbound.
"""

import collections
import copy
import fcntl
import ipaddress
import json
import os
import re
import select
import shlex
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request

ENV = os.environ.get
ROOT = (ENV("TUXWALL_TRIPWIRE_ROOT") or "").rstrip("/")
NETNS = ENV("TUXWALL_TRIPWIRE_NETNS") or ""


def _p(path):
    return ROOT + path if ROOT else path


CONFIG = _p("/etc/tuxwall/tripwire.json")
